<Base path="/redact-image/" title="Redact Images Manually — Black Box, Pixelate & Blur | PlainMile" description="Manually cover text or faces with a black box, mosaic or blur. Preview actual effects, undo and download JPG, PNG, WebP or batch ZIP locally in your browser.">
<section class="page-head"><p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Image tools</a> / Redact images</p><h1>Manually redact images and screenshots</h1><p class="lead">Draw rectangles to hide sensitive areas. Choose solid black, pixelation or Gaussian blur, then download. No automatic text detection.</p></section>
<Redactor client:load lang="en"/>
<section class="reference">
<h2>How to redact a screenshot</h2><ol><li>Select or paste one or more JPG, PNG or WebP images.</li><li>Wait for the selected image preview, then drag over it to draw a cover. Use Remove, Undo or Redo to correct it.</li><li>Choose black box for private data, pixelation for visual obscuring or blur for a soft effect. Preview and adjust strength.</li><li>Review each image and download a new image or ZIP.</li></ol>
<h2>Black box, pixelation or blur?</h2><p>A solid black box replaces source pixels in the selected area and is the safer choice for passwords, personal details and account numbers. Pixelation and Gaussian blur can still leak recognizable information, so do not rely on them for confidential content.</p>
<h2>Does the tool automatically detect text?</h2><p>No. This tool is manual only. Draw exactly the areas that you want covered. There is no OCR scan or automatic selection.</p>
<h2>Batch export and privacy</h2><p>New uploads are added to the list without removing previously drawn covers. Remove one accidentally selected image without clearing other images or their boxes; an image with redaction boxes asks for confirmation before deletion. Clearing the full batch also asks for confirmation when it would discard redactions. Each image has independent covers and Undo/Redo. Every image must have a cover before being included in a ZIP. Animated PNG/WebP files are rejected, because exporting only their first frame would be misleading. Exports are re-encoded without source metadata or source filenames. Switching between images without modifying boxes keeps completed downloads available. If source transparency matters, export as PNG or WebP: JPG replaces transparent pixels with white. Everything runs in your browser, without uploading image contents. Limits: 12 images, 100 MB total, 32 megapixels per image and 96 megapixels combined, and 128 MB of total output image data per ZIP. If a batch exceeds the output limit, split it or export as JPG/WebP.</p>
<h2>Related tools</h2><ul class="related-links"><li><a href="/resize-image/">Resize image</a></li><li><a href="/compress-image-to-size/">Compress to KB</a></li><li><a href="/bulk-image-resizer/">Bulk resize</a></li></ul>
</section></Base>
